{"name":"White Cliffs","hex":"#e8e3c9","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"White Cliffs","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Proper noun","definitions":["A locality in the Central Darling council area and the Unincorporated Far West Area, western New South Wales, Australia."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/White_Cliffs"}],"entries":[{"title":"White Cliffs of Dover","blocks":[{"text":"The White Cliffs of Dover are the region of English coastline facing the Strait of Dover and France. The cliff face, which reaches a height of 350 feet (110 m), owes its striking appearance to its composition of chalk accented by streaks of black flint, deposited during the Late Cretaceous. The cliffs, on both sides of the town of Dover in Kent, stretch for eight miles (13 km). The White Cliffs of Dover form part of the North Downs. The cliffs are part of the Dover to Kingsdown Cliffs Site of Special Scientific Interest and Special Area of Conservation. The top of the cliffs hosts a chalk grassland ecosystem with an abundance of bird, flower, and butterfly species."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/White_Cliffs_of_Dover_02.JPG/330px-White_Cliffs_of_Dover_02.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White_Cliffs_of_Dover","wikidata":"Q754785"},{"title":"(There'll Be Bluebirds Over) The White Cliffs of Dover","blocks":[{"text":"\"(There'll Be Bluebirds Over) The White Cliffs of Dover\" is a popular American World War II song composed in 1941 by American composer Walter Kent to lyrics by American lyricist Nat Burton. Made famous in the United Kingdom by British singer Vera Lynn's 1942 version, it was one of Lynn's best-known recordings and among the most popular World War II tunes."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/(There'll_Be_Bluebirds_Over)_The_White_Cliffs_of_Dover","wikidata":"Q3437295"},{"title":"Cliff","blocks":[{"text":"In geography and geology, a cliff or bluff or rock face is an area of rock which has a general angle defined by the vertical, or nearly vertical. Cliffs are formed by the processes of weathering and erosion, with the effect of gravity. Cliffs are common on coasts, in mountainous areas, escarpments and along rivers. Cliffs are usually composed of rock that is resistant to weathering and erosion. The sedimentary rocks that are most likely to form cliffs include sandstone, limestone, chalk, and dolomite. Igneous rocks such as granite and basalt also often form cliffs."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/74/White_Cliffs_of_Dover_02.JPG/330px-White_Cliffs_of_Dover_02.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cliff","wikidata":"Q107679"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White","wikidata":"Q23444"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Dover989.JPG/500px-Dover989.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Dover989.JPG/1280px-Dover989.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Dover989.JPG/500px-Dover989.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The cliffs' multiple layers of flint match those seen across the channel at Cap Gris Nez, France, evidence of a land connection between England and France in prehistoric times.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ADover989.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Cliffs_of_Dover_erosion.jpg/500px-Cliffs_of_Dover_erosion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Cliffs_of_Dover_erosion.jpg/500px-Cliffs_of_Dover_erosion.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Evidence of erosion along the cliff top","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ACliffs_of_Dover_erosion.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Landslide_near_Dover_Harbour.jpg/500px-Landslide_near_Dover_Harbour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Landslide_near_Dover_Harbour.jpg/1280px-Landslide_near_Dover_Harbour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Landslide_near_Dover_Harbour.jpg/500px-Landslide_near_Dover_Harbour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Landslide near Dover Harbour, June 2012","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALandslide_near_Dover_Harbour.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Exmoors_on_Exmoor.jpg/500px-Exmoors_on_Exmoor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Exmoors_on_Exmoor.jpg/1280px-Exmoors_on_Exmoor.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dd/Exmoors_on_Exmoor.jpg/5